.TH RENAME-SIMPLE 1 "2026-08-12" "rename-simple 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rename-simple \- clean filenames to lowercase, ASCII-safe slugs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rename-simple
[\fB\-f\fR | \fB\-d\fR]
[\fB\-U\fR]
[\fB\-H\fR]
[\fB\-A\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-V\fR]
\fIfiles\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rename-simple
renames files and/or directories by normalising accented characters, replacing
spaces and special characters with hyphens, and lowercasing everything.
.PP
Each \fIfiles\fR argument is an entry that is renamed \fBitself\fR (like the
traditional \fBrename\fR(1) command): the program acts on the arguments you
give it, not on their contents. Globbing is left to the shell, so
\fBrename-simple\fR \fI*.jpg\fR renames every matching file, and (with a shell
that supports recursive globs, e.g. \fBzsh\fR or \fBbash\fR with
\fIglobstar\fR) \fBrename-simple\fR \fIdir/**/*.pdf\fR cleans a whole subtree.
.PP
Both files and directories are renamed by default; \fB\-f\fR / \fB\-d\fR act as
a type filter on the arguments. When \fBno\fR argument is given, the program
prints its help and exits.
.PP
The transformation pipeline is:
.IP 1. 4
Transliterate accented and non-ASCII characters to their ASCII equivalents.
This covers Latin scripts (e.g. \[u00E9] -> e, \[u00DF] -> ss,
\[u00C6] -> ae), typographic ligatures and compatibility forms
(\[uFB01] -> fi, fullwidth letters, superscripts, \[u2122] -> tm), and Greek
and Cyrillic letters through a simple romanisation
(\[u039B] -> l, \[u0416] -> zh).
.IP 2. 4
Replace all remaining special characters with a hyphen (\fB\-\fR).
.IP 3. 4
Collapse consecutive hyphens into a single hyphen.
.IP 4. 4
Clean underscore/hyphen boundaries (\fI_\-\fR and \fI\-_\fR become \fI_\fR).
.IP 5. 4
Collapse consecutive underscores.
.IP 6. 4
Strip leading and trailing hyphens and underscores.
.PP
The optional cleanup fixes (\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-H\fR, \fB\-A\fR) run \fBbefore\fR
this pipeline: they repair the name (mojibake, HTML markup) so that the slug
is computed from the intended text rather than from the damaged one.
.PP
Compound extensions (\fI.tar.gz\fR, \fI.tar.bz2\fR, \fI.tar.xz\fR,
\fI.tar.zst\fR) are preserved. An extension is only kept separate when it
consists entirely of ASCII alphanumeric characters and is at most 10 characters
long. Extensions containing accents, spaces, punctuation or exceeding 10
characters are re-absorbed into the filename stem and processed through the
full transliteration pipeline.
.PP
Hidden files (names starting with a dot) are never modified.
.PP
By default the program prints one line per renamed entry (\fB[R]\fR) or error
(\fB[E]\fR) followed by a one-line summary. Use \fB\-v\fR/\fB\-\-verbose\fR to
also list untouched entries (\fB[X]\fR), or \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR to
suppress all output. Marks and arrows are colourised when the output is a
terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-files\-only\fR
Rename files only.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-dirs\-only\fR
Rename directories only.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-unicode\fR
Repair mojibake before renaming: names whose UTF-8 bytes were wrongly decoded
as Latin-1 or Windows-1252 (e.g. \fICafÃ©.txt\fR is treated as
\fICaf\[u00E9].txt\fR and becomes \fIcafe.txt\fR). The repair is all-or-nothing:
a name that is not fully re-decodable is left untouched, so correctly named
files are never corrupted. Double mojibake is handled.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-html\fR
Strip HTML tags and decode HTML entities before renaming (e.g.
\fI<b>Tom &amp; Jerry.mp4\fR is treated as \fITom & Jerry.mp4\fR and becomes
\fItom\-jerry.mp4\fR). Block\-level tags (\fI<br>\fR, \fI<p>\fR, \fI<div>\fR,
\fI<li>\fR, \fI<h1>\fR\[en]\fI<h6>\fR, ...) are replaced by a space so the
surrounding words stay separated (\fIdata<br>client\fR becomes
\fIdata\-client\fR), while inline tags (\fI<b>\fR, \fI<i>\fR, \fI<span>\fR, ...)
are removed with no gap (\fIclient<b>s\fR becomes \fIclients\fR). Named
(\fI&eacute;\fR), decimal (\fI&#233;\fR) and hexadecimal (\fI&#xE9;\fR) entities
are supported; anything that does not parse as a tag or entity is kept verbatim.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-all\fR
Apply every cleanup fix. Currently equivalent to \fB\-U\fR \fB\-H\fR; future
cleanup fixes will be folded into this option.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing at all.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show every entry, including the ones left untouched, plus the summary.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be renamed without actually renaming any entry.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All entries were renamed successfully (or no entries needed renaming).
Per-entry failures (missing entry, conflict, I/O error) are reported on
standard error but do not change the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (invalid command-line arguments).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Rename specific files (the arguments themselves)
.B rename-simple
"Mon Fichier.txt" *.jpg
.TP
Rename both files and directories in the current directory
.B rename-simple
*
.TP
Rename files only, with verbose output
.B rename-simple
\fB\-f\fR \fB\-v\fR ~/Downloads/*
.TP
Preview what would be renamed without touching anything
.B rename-simple
\fB\-n\fR \fB\-v\fR *
.TP
Repair mojibake and strip HTML markup while renaming
.B rename-simple
\fB\-A\fR ~/Downloads/*
.TP
Rename every PDF under a directory (zsh / bash globstar)
.B rename-simple
~/Documents/**/*.pdf
.TP
Rename directories only
.B rename-simple
\fB\-d\fR *
